{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2197-06-22 19:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee. I understand you’ve been feeling very nauseous and vomiting since this morning?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2197-06-22 19:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I woke up feeling awful. I’ve thrown up greenish fluid several times. I tried to take my pills but I just vomited them right back up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2197-06-22 19:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You mentioned you’ve had this happen before—about 10 times in the last three months?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2197-06-22 19:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Exactly. But after my last hospital stay, my GI doctor adjusted my gastric pacer and gave me new meds. I was fine for a full week—then this hit again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2197-06-22 19:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s frustrating. Your vitals show high blood pressure and a fast heart rate, which we often see during these episodes. We’ll start IV fluids and give you some anti-nausea meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2197-06-22 19:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will that be enough? Last time, nothing helped until they adjusted the pacer."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2197-06-22 19:11:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re giving you Zofran, Reglan, and Compazine now. But we’ll also check if there’s another issue. An abdominal X-ray was done—no blockage, which is good."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2197-06-22 19:13:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. My blood sugar was high earlier—should I be worried?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2197-06-22 19:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your fingerstick glucose was 349. Since you’re not eating, we’ve started a lower dose of Lantus insulin and will use Humalog as needed. We’ll monitor it closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2197-06-23 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs from last night were stable—no infection in blood cultures, kidney function is normal. But your heart rate is still in the 110s."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2197-06-23 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel anxious, but I know it’s from being so sick. Is the fast heart rate dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2197-06-23 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s likely a stress response to nausea and vomiting. It usually improves when symptoms do. We’re watching it on telemetry, and your ECG shows just sinus tachycardia—no heart damage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2197-06-23 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. What’s next?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2197-06-23 08:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re considering an endoscopy. Since you’ve had esophagitis before, we want to check if that’s flaring up or if the pylorus is too tight, slowing stomach emptying."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2197-06-23 08:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I had that last time. They found esophagitis and adjusted my pacer. Could we do something more this time?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2197-06-23 08:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. If the pylorus is tight, we can inject Botox to relax it. It’s been helpful for some patients with gastroparesis like yours."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2197-06-23 08:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That sounds worth trying. When would it happen?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2197-06-23 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve scheduled it for tomorrow morning. For now, we’ll keep you on IV fluids and continue the anti-nausea meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2197-06-24 07:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your blood pressure is still high—188/88. We gave hydralazine yesterday, but it’s not fully controlled. We’ll restart your lisinopril once you can keep pills down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2197-06-24 07:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been on lisinopril for years. Should I be concerned it’s not working?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2197-06-24 07:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s likely because you haven’t been able to take it. Once we restart it and your nausea improves, we expect your BP to come down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2197-06-24 07:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Makes sense. Any update on the endoscopy?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2197-06-24 07:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Still on for today. We’ll also repeat your chest X-ray to check your port and lungs. You had a portable one last night—heart size is normal, lungs are clear."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2197-06-26 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re about to take you for your endoscopy. You’ll be sedated, and the GI team will look at your esophagus and stomach."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2197-06-26 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And they’ll do the Botox if needed?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2197-06-26 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, if they see the pylorus is tight. We’ll update you when you wake up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2197-06-26 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The endoscopy went well. You had some inflammation and a small ulcer at the bottom of your esophagus—consistent with reflux. The stomach was empty, which confirms delayed emptying."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2197-06-26 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the Botox?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2197-06-26 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, they injected Botox into the pylorus to help it relax. This should help food move from your stomach into the intestine more easily."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2197-06-26 14:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. Do I need to stay on the same meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2197-06-26 14:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep you on Protonix twice daily for the esophagitis, and continue Reglan and erythromycin to help stomach motility. Your nausea should start improving."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2197-06-27 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—you made it through the night without vomiting. We’re going to try advancing your diet to clear liquids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2197-06-27 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m actually feeling less nauseous. Could the Botox be working already?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2197-06-27 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s possible. It can take a few days, but some patients feel better quickly. We’ll monitor you closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2197-06-27 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My heart rate is still high—118. Should I worry?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2197-06-27 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s still elevated, but it’s coming down slowly. As your nausea improves, we expect your heart rate and blood pressure to normalize."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2197-06-27 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You tolerated clear liquids well. We’re going to try a soft diabetic diet—mashed potatoes, applesauce, things like that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2197-06-27 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m hungry for real food. Can I have insulin with it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2197-06-27 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ll restart your full insulin regimen—Lantus at night and Humalog before meals. Your glucose was 153 this morning—well controlled."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2197-06-27 14:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. Any other labs?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2197-06-27 14:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your potassium was low yesterday—2.9—so we gave supplements. This morning it’s 3.4, which is better but still a bit low. We’ll keep giving potassium with your meals."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2197-06-27 14:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I didn’t realize that could happen. Is it from vomiting?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2197-06-27 14:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Losing fluids through vomiting can deplete potassium. We’re replacing it, so it should normalize soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2197-06-28 07:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve had two meals and no vomiting. Your heart rate is down to 98, and BP is 138/82. We think you’re ready to go home today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2197-06-28 07:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s wonderful. Are my meds the same?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2197-06-28 07:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes—same doses. Continue Protonix twice daily, Reglan four times a day, erythromycin three times a day, and your insulin. Use Zofran or the suppository if nausea returns."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2197-06-28 07:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2197-06-28 07:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "See your GI doctor in one week to check how you’re doing with the Botox. Also follow up with your endocrinologist for diabetes management."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2197-06-28 07:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What if I start vomiting again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2197-06-28 07:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Call your doctor or come back if you can’t keep food or liquids down, or if you have chest pain, high fever, or vomit blood."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2197-06-28 07:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you for getting me through this episode."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2197-06-28 07:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing great. The Botox may help reduce how often this happens. We’ll keep working to get you more stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2197-06-28 07:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I really hope so. Ten admissions in three months is too much."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2197-06-28 07:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re all on your side. Let’s aim for fewer trips back. Your husband can pick you up around noon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2197-06-28 07:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "He’ll be relieved. Thanks again, Dr. Lee."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2197-06-28 07:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care. We’ll see you in follow-up if needed, but I hope not!"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2197-06-28 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just checking in—how are you feeling after breakfast?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2197-06-28 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Still no nausea. I ate most of my oatmeal and drank tea. I think I’m really turning a corner."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2197-06-28 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s excellent. We’ll finalize your discharge paperwork soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2197-06-28 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m so grateful. This episode was scary, but I feel much better now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2197-06-28 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re glad too. Keep up with your diet, meds, and follow-up. You’ve made great progress."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2197-06-28 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Nurse will come by to review your medications and discharge instructions one more time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2197-06-28 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ve got my list. I’ll take everything as prescribed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2197-06-28 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "One last thing—your discharge labs show mild anemia and low potassium, but both are improving. No need for transfusion or extra treatment now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2197-06-28 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Should I take iron or anything?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2197-06-28 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not unless your outpatient doctor recommends it. Focus on nutrition and follow-up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2197-06-28 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will do. Thank you for everything."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2197-06-22 18:45:00",
    "admission_end": "2197-06-28 14:46:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 30-year-old woman with Type 1 diabetes and gastroparesis s/p gastric pacer was admitted for severe nausea and bilious vomiting, unable to tolerate oral intake or medications. She had been recently discharged after pacer adjustment but re-presented one week later with recurrent symptoms. Initial management included IV fluids, antiemetics, and insulin adjustment for hyperglycemia. Hypertension and tachycardia were present, likely due to sympathetic activation from nausea. An abdominal X-ray ruled out obstruction, and blood cultures were negative. An endoscopy revealed esophagitis and a tight pylorus, leading to Botox injection for sphincter relaxation. Post-procedure, her nausea improved, allowing advancement to a soft diet. Potassium was repleted for hypokalemia. She was discharged home in stable condition on her prior medications, with follow-up scheduled with GI and endocrinology.",
    "problems": [
      "Diabetes with neurological manifestations, type I [juvenile type], uncontrolled",
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Persistent vomiting",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Esophagitis, unspecified",
      "Hypopotassemia",
      "Acetonuria"
    ]
  }
}
